1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,655
You push your code.

2
00:00:01,655 --> 00:00:02,688
Nobody builds it.

3
00:00:02,688 --> 00:00:03,661
Nobody tests it.

4
00:00:03,661 --> 00:00:04,816
It just sits there,

5
00:00:04,816 --> 00:00:06,153
and you hope it works.

6
00:00:06,703 --> 00:00:07,612
Two days later,

7
00:00:07,612 --> 00:00:09,369
a teammate pulls your change,

8
00:00:09,369 --> 00:00:11,853
and the build is broken on their machine.

9
00:00:11,853 --> 00:00:12,398
On yours,

10
00:00:12,398 --> 00:00:13,125
it was fine.

11
00:00:13,675 --> 00:00:16,937
What if every push built your code and ran your tests,

12
00:00:16,937 --> 00:00:18,085
on a fresh machine,

13
00:00:18,085 --> 00:00:18,689
by itself,

14
00:00:18,689 --> 00:00:19,777
in under a minute,

15
00:00:19,777 --> 00:00:21,227
and told you the result?

16
00:00:21,777 --> 00:00:23,171
That is GitHub Actions.

17
00:00:23,171 --> 00:00:25,355
Today you build your first pipeline,

18
00:00:25,355 --> 00:00:27,174
and you watch it run for real.

19
00:00:28,724 --> 00:00:30,153
Here is what to expect.

20
00:00:30,703 --> 00:00:35,589
One: six words you must know. Event, workflow, job, runner, step, and action.

21
00:00:36,139 --> 00:00:37,263
Two: a live demo,

22
00:00:37,263 --> 00:00:39,579
in real GitHub and a real terminal.

23
00:00:39,579 --> 00:00:41,630
We write the smallest workflow,

24
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:43,814
then a real build for Cloud Cart,

25
00:00:43,814 --> 00:00:45,269
and we watch both run.

26
00:00:45,819 --> 00:00:49,702
Three: we break a test on purpose, find the first error, and fix it.

27
00:00:50,252 --> 00:00:53,900
Four: the mistakes beginners make, and where to find the lab files.

28
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:59,354
Six words. Learn them once, and every Actions page will make sense.

29
00:00:59,904 --> 00:01:03,528
An event is something that happens in your repository.

30
00:01:03,528 --> 00:01:03,998
A push.

31
00:01:03,998 --> 00:01:05,005
A pull request.

32
00:01:05,005 --> 00:01:05,877
Even a timer.

33
00:01:06,427 --> 00:01:10,041
A workflow is a file that says: when this event happens,

34
00:01:10,041 --> 00:01:10,880
do this work.

35
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:13,267
It sits in a folder named dot github,

36
00:01:13,267 --> 00:01:14,299
slash workflows.

37
00:01:14,849 --> 00:01:20,588
A job is a group of steps that runs on one machine. A workflow can have one job, or many.

38
00:01:21,138 --> 00:01:22,694
A runner is that machine.

39
00:01:22,694 --> 00:01:25,372
GitHub gives you a fresh one for every job,

40
00:01:25,372 --> 00:01:27,239
and throws it away afterwards.

41
00:01:27,789 --> 00:01:32,973
A step is one small task inside a job. It is either a shell command, or an action.

42
00:01:33,523 --> 00:01:36,924
And an action is a reusable step that someone else wrote,

43
00:01:36,924 --> 00:01:37,759
like checkout,

44
00:01:37,759 --> 00:01:40,265
which downloads your code onto the runner.

45
00:01:40,815 --> 00:01:41,190
Event,

46
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:41,754
workflow,

47
00:01:41,754 --> 00:01:42,004
job,

48
00:01:42,004 --> 00:01:42,442
runner,

49
00:01:42,442 --> 00:01:42,756
step,

50
00:01:42,756 --> 00:01:43,194
action.

51
00:01:43,194 --> 00:01:44,634
Say them in that order,

52
00:01:44,634 --> 00:01:46,575
and you have the whole picture.

53
00:01:50,125 --> 00:01:51,818
Let us build it for real.

54
00:01:51,818 --> 00:01:53,918
This is a repository on GitHub,

55
00:01:53,918 --> 00:01:55,950
called cloud cart actions lab.

56
00:01:55,950 --> 00:01:58,253
It holds the Cloud Cart cart core,

57
00:01:58,253 --> 00:01:59,811
our Java shopping cart,

58
00:01:59,811 --> 00:02:00,962
and nothing else.

59
00:02:01,512 --> 00:02:03,362
Across the top are the tabs.

60
00:02:03,362 --> 00:02:04,617
Code is your files.

61
00:02:04,617 --> 00:02:07,392
Issues and Pull requests are for teamwork.

62
00:02:07,392 --> 00:02:09,704
And Actions is where pipelines run.

63
00:02:10,254 --> 00:02:11,337
We open Actions.

64
00:02:11,337 --> 00:02:12,150
It is empty,

65
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:13,978
so GitHub offers templates.

66
00:02:13,978 --> 00:02:15,467
We will write our own,

67
00:02:15,467 --> 00:02:18,852
because the smallest workflow is only a few lines.

68
00:02:19,402 --> 00:02:20,522
Now the terminal,

69
00:02:20,522 --> 00:02:21,774
inside the project.

70
00:02:21,774 --> 00:02:23,487
A workflow is just a file,

71
00:02:23,487 --> 00:02:25,332
so first we make its folder.

72
00:02:25,882 --> 00:02:30,640
Then the file, called hello dot Y M L. We explain each line as we type it.

73
00:02:31,190 --> 00:02:35,200
Name: Hello. This is the label you will see on the Actions page.

74
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:40,934
On: push. That is the event. Every push to this repository starts the workflow.

75
00:02:41,484 --> 00:02:44,343
Jobs, and one job, which we name say hello.

76
00:02:44,893 --> 00:02:50,738
Runs on ubuntu latest. That is the runner: a fresh Linux machine, owned by GitHub.

77
00:02:51,288 --> 00:02:55,896
Steps. One step, and it runs a command: echo, hello from a GitHub machine.

78
00:02:58,146 --> 00:03:02,370
Eight lines. Now git add, to stage the file, and git commit, to save it.

79
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:06,888
And git push. The push is the event, so GitHub starts the workflow.

80
00:03:12,438 --> 00:03:16,001
Back in Chrome, the Actions tab. A run appeared by itself.

81
00:03:16,551 --> 00:03:21,052
Yellow means it is waiting for a machine. Then a spinner, running. And then green.

82
00:03:23,102 --> 00:03:24,289
Each row is one run.

83
00:03:24,289 --> 00:03:25,890
It shows the workflow name,

84
00:03:25,890 --> 00:03:27,433
the event that started it,

85
00:03:27,433 --> 00:03:28,085
the branch,

86
00:03:28,085 --> 00:03:28,619
the time,

87
00:03:28,619 --> 00:03:29,865
and how long it took.

88
00:03:30,415 --> 00:03:31,354
We open the run.

89
00:03:31,354 --> 00:03:33,466
The summary shows what triggered it,

90
00:03:33,466 --> 00:03:34,111
the status,

91
00:03:34,111 --> 00:03:34,874
and the jobs,

92
00:03:34,874 --> 00:03:35,578
on the left.

93
00:03:36,128 --> 00:03:37,222
We open the job.

94
00:03:37,222 --> 00:03:38,042
Three steps.

95
00:03:38,042 --> 00:03:38,795
Set up job,

96
00:03:38,795 --> 00:03:39,957
and Complete job,

97
00:03:39,957 --> 00:03:41,667
which GitHub adds itself.

98
00:03:41,667 --> 00:03:42,897
And in the middle,

99
00:03:42,897 --> 00:03:43,786
our own step.

100
00:03:44,336 --> 00:03:47,715
GitHub shows the log text only when you are signed in.

101
00:03:47,715 --> 00:03:48,904
So in the terminal,

102
00:03:48,904 --> 00:03:50,843
one command shows the same log.

103
00:03:51,393 --> 00:03:52,471
Here is our line,

104
00:03:52,471 --> 00:03:55,260
and the output: hello from a GitHub machine.

105
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:58,936
A machine that did not exist a minute ago ran our command,

106
00:03:58,936 --> 00:04:00,204
and was thrown away.

107
00:04:00,754 --> 00:04:02,849
That was the smallest workflow.

108
00:04:02,849 --> 00:04:06,026
Now a real one: it builds and tests Cloud Cart.

109
00:04:06,026 --> 00:04:09,203
We copy the new file into the workflows folder.

110
00:04:09,203 --> 00:04:11,163
We remove the hello workflow,

111
00:04:11,163 --> 00:04:12,717
because it did its job.

112
00:04:12,717 --> 00:04:13,326
Then add,

113
00:04:13,326 --> 00:04:13,799
commit,

114
00:04:13,799 --> 00:04:14,407
and push.

115
00:04:17,857 --> 00:04:20,972
On GitHub, we open the file. Only eighteen lines.

116
00:04:26,122 --> 00:04:28,298
Line one: name. We call it CI.

117
00:04:28,848 --> 00:04:32,901
Then on. We said: run on a push to main, and on every pull request.

118
00:04:33,451 --> 00:04:35,798
Then jobs. One job, named build.

119
00:04:36,348 --> 00:04:40,124
Runs on ubuntu latest. The same fresh Linux runner.

120
00:04:40,674 --> 00:04:43,852
Then steps. A list. Each item starts with a dash.

121
00:04:44,402 --> 00:04:46,720
Step one uses actions slash checkout.

122
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:47,409
Without it,

123
00:04:47,409 --> 00:04:48,662
the runner is empty,

124
00:04:48,662 --> 00:04:50,290
and has none of your code.

125
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:52,749
Step two uses setup java,

126
00:04:52,749 --> 00:04:54,352
and asks for Temurin,

127
00:04:54,352 --> 00:04:55,725
version seventeen.

128
00:04:55,725 --> 00:04:59,160
The runner needs a Java before Maven can run.

129
00:04:59,710 --> 00:05:02,341
Step three is a plain command: mvn,

130
00:05:02,341 --> 00:05:02,867
dash B,

131
00:05:02,867 --> 00:05:03,393
verify.

132
00:05:03,393 --> 00:05:05,196
Dash B means batch mode,

133
00:05:05,196 --> 00:05:07,150
which keeps the log clean.

134
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:09,029
Verify compiles the code,

135
00:05:09,029 --> 00:05:10,156
runs the tests,

136
00:05:10,156 --> 00:05:11,358
and packages it.

137
00:05:11,908 --> 00:05:14,476
And indentation matters in Y A M L.

138
00:05:14,476 --> 00:05:15,282
Two spaces,

139
00:05:15,282 --> 00:05:16,089
never tabs.

140
00:05:16,089 --> 00:05:18,436
Most first errors are an indent.

141
00:05:18,986 --> 00:05:24,341
Back to the Actions tab. Two runs now: the hello run from before, and our new CI.

142
00:05:24,891 --> 00:05:26,260
We open the CI run,

143
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:27,486
and then the job.

144
00:05:27,486 --> 00:05:29,432
Now the steps do real work.

145
00:05:29,432 --> 00:05:30,369
Get the code.

146
00:05:30,369 --> 00:05:31,234
Set up Java.

147
00:05:31,234 --> 00:05:32,315
Build and test.

148
00:05:34,465 --> 00:05:35,368
While it runs,

149
00:05:35,368 --> 00:05:37,302
think about what is happening.

150
00:05:37,302 --> 00:05:39,817
GitHub started a fresh virtual machine.

151
00:05:39,817 --> 00:05:41,365
It downloaded your code.

152
00:05:41,365 --> 00:05:42,526
It installed Java.

153
00:05:42,526 --> 00:05:45,750
And Maven is now downloading what the build needs,

154
00:05:45,750 --> 00:05:47,878
because the machine starts empty,

155
00:05:47,878 --> 00:05:48,588
every time.

156
00:05:49,138 --> 00:05:53,404
Green. The whole pipeline, well under a minute, with no one touching a keyboard.

157
00:05:54,754 --> 00:05:59,277
Let us see the test result. In the terminal, we show only the lines that matter.

158
00:06:00,727 --> 00:06:05,548
Tests run: one. Failures: zero. And BUILD SUCCESS. That is the evidence.

159
00:06:06,098 --> 00:06:08,856
Now the part that makes a pipeline useful.

160
00:06:08,856 --> 00:06:10,038
A test that fails.

161
00:06:10,038 --> 00:06:12,731
We change the expected total in our test,

162
00:06:12,731 --> 00:06:14,635
from twenty nine ninety nine,

163
00:06:14,635 --> 00:06:15,817
to three thousand.

164
00:06:15,817 --> 00:06:17,131
A wrong expectation,

165
00:06:17,131 --> 00:06:17,853
on purpose.

166
00:06:20,403 --> 00:06:24,798
Git diff shows exactly what changed: one line. We commit, and we push.

167
00:06:27,348 --> 00:06:31,166
In Chrome, the Actions tab. The run starts again by itself.

168
00:06:33,516 --> 00:06:34,405
While we wait,

169
00:06:34,405 --> 00:06:36,309
remember what GitHub is doing.

170
00:06:36,309 --> 00:06:38,213
It starts a brand new machine.

171
00:06:38,213 --> 00:06:40,054
It fetches this exact commit.

172
00:06:40,054 --> 00:06:41,513
It runs the same steps,

173
00:06:41,513 --> 00:06:42,656
in the same order.

174
00:06:42,656 --> 00:06:45,258
Nothing is left over from the run before.

175
00:06:45,258 --> 00:06:48,114
That is why a pipeline gives the same answer,

176
00:06:48,114 --> 00:06:48,812
every time.

177
00:06:51,962 --> 00:06:53,690
And this time, a red cross.

178
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:01,255
We open the job.

179
00:07:01,255 --> 00:07:02,650
Build and test is red.

180
00:07:02,650 --> 00:07:04,933
And the steps after it were skipped,

181
00:07:04,933 --> 00:07:07,216
because a failed step stops the job.

182
00:07:10,366 --> 00:07:11,483
Which test failed,

183
00:07:11,483 --> 00:07:11,979
and why?

184
00:07:11,979 --> 00:07:13,405
The log has the answer.

185
00:07:13,405 --> 00:07:14,398
In the terminal,

186
00:07:14,398 --> 00:07:16,382
we ask for the failed step only.

187
00:07:18,432 --> 00:07:21,585
Here is the first error: expected three thousand,

188
00:07:21,585 --> 00:07:23,644
but was twenty nine ninety nine.

189
00:07:23,644 --> 00:07:25,059
The test names itself,

190
00:07:25,059 --> 00:07:26,539
and shows both numbers.

191
00:07:27,089 --> 00:07:31,889
That is the habit. Do not guess. Find the first error, read it, and change one thing.

192
00:07:32,439 --> 00:07:33,939
We fix it the clean way.

193
00:07:33,939 --> 00:07:36,001
Git revert undoes our bad commit,

194
00:07:36,001 --> 00:07:37,813
and keeps the history honest.

195
00:07:37,813 --> 00:07:38,626
Then we push.

196
00:07:39,876 --> 00:07:41,326
In Chrome, the next run.

197
00:07:45,976 --> 00:07:47,015
The same pipeline,

198
00:07:47,015 --> 00:07:48,457
on another fresh machine.

199
00:07:48,457 --> 00:07:50,130
It fetches the revert commit,

200
00:07:50,130 --> 00:07:50,880
sets up Java,

201
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:52,322
and runs the tests again.

202
00:07:52,322 --> 00:07:55,264
This time the test expects twenty nine ninety nine,

203
00:07:55,264 --> 00:07:56,302
so it should pass.

204
00:07:56,302 --> 00:07:58,033
And notice what we did not do.

205
00:07:58,033 --> 00:07:59,821
We did not edit the old commit,

206
00:07:59,821 --> 00:08:01,783
and we did not delete the red run.

207
00:08:01,783 --> 00:08:03,513
The history keeps the mistake,

208
00:08:03,513 --> 00:08:04,205
and the fix,

209
00:08:04,205 --> 00:08:04,955
side by side.

210
00:08:04,955 --> 00:08:05,994
Nothing is hidden,

211
00:08:05,994 --> 00:08:08,070
and you can always see what changed,

212
00:08:08,070 --> 00:08:08,590
and when.

213
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:11,185
And it is green.

214
00:08:16,435 --> 00:08:17,549
Look at the list.

215
00:08:17,549 --> 00:08:17,942
Green,

216
00:08:17,942 --> 00:08:18,531
then red,

217
00:08:18,531 --> 00:08:19,252
then green.

218
00:08:19,252 --> 00:08:20,824
Every push has a result,

219
00:08:20,824 --> 00:08:24,755
and every result is tied to the exact commit that caused it.

220
00:08:25,305 --> 00:08:27,232
That is continuous integration.

221
00:08:27,232 --> 00:08:29,284
Every change is built and tested,

222
00:08:29,284 --> 00:08:30,465
on a clean machine,

223
00:08:30,465 --> 00:08:31,460
within a minute,

224
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:33,262
and the team sees the answer.

225
00:08:34,812 --> 00:08:36,988
Beginner mistakes, and how to spot them.

226
00:08:37,538 --> 00:08:41,594
The workflow does not appear: the file must be inside dot github,

227
00:08:41,594 --> 00:08:42,593
slash workflows,

228
00:08:42,593 --> 00:08:44,215
and on the default branch.

229
00:08:44,765 --> 00:08:50,419
The run fails at once with a syntax error: look at the indentation. Spaces, not tabs.

230
00:08:50,969 --> 00:08:56,067
Maven says there is no pom: you forgot the checkout step, so the runner has no code.

231
00:08:56,617 --> 00:09:01,332
Wrong Java version: set the version in setup java, to match your project.

232
00:09:01,882 --> 00:09:05,802
And nothing runs on a push: check the branch name in the on section.

233
00:09:05,802 --> 00:09:07,877
It must match the branch you pushed.

234
00:09:09,427 --> 00:09:12,222
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

235
00:09:12,772 --> 00:09:21,006
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai, in the folder academy/lab.

236
00:09:21,556 --> 00:09:27,487
Two: the files, hello dot Y M L, and CI dot Y M L, and the Cloud Cart cart core.

237
00:09:28,037 --> 00:09:32,624
Three: make your own free GitHub repository, copy the files in, and push.

238
00:09:33,174 --> 00:09:36,156
Four: public repositories get free runner minutes.

239
00:09:36,156 --> 00:09:38,720
Private ones have a free monthly allowance.

240
00:09:39,270 --> 00:09:44,241
Five: when you finish, delete the practice repository. Nothing here needs to stay.

241
00:09:44,791 --> 00:09:46,434
The link is in the description.

242
00:09:47,984 --> 00:09:50,224
Your turn, in your own repository.

243
00:09:50,774 --> 00:09:55,254
One: write the hello workflow, push it, and find your run in the Actions tab.

244
00:09:55,804 --> 00:10:00,220
Two: add the CI workflow for a Maven project, and get a green run.

245
00:10:00,770 --> 00:10:04,845
Three: break a test on purpose. Find the first error in the failed step.

246
00:10:05,395 --> 00:10:09,192
Four: fix it with git revert, push, and watch it turn green.

247
00:10:09,742 --> 00:10:12,387
Five: delete the repository when you are done.

248
00:10:12,937 --> 00:10:14,665
Pause the video, and do it now.

249
00:10:30,215 --> 00:10:31,090
Quick check.

250
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:33,453
Which folder holds your workflows?

251
00:10:34,003 --> 00:10:36,136
A step failed. Where do you look first?

252
00:10:36,686 --> 00:10:38,734
Why does a workflow need the checkout step?

253
00:10:39,284 --> 00:10:41,887
A runner finished the job. What happens to it?

254
00:10:42,437 --> 00:10:44,778
Objectives covered: M C twenty,

255
00:10:44,778 --> 00:10:45,836
objective one,

256
00:10:45,836 --> 00:10:46,591
workflows,

257
00:10:46,591 --> 00:10:47,119
events,

258
00:10:47,119 --> 00:10:47,497
jobs,

259
00:10:47,497 --> 00:10:47,950
steps,

260
00:10:47,950 --> 00:10:48,554
actions,

261
00:10:48,554 --> 00:10:49,461
and runners.

262
00:10:49,461 --> 00:10:50,820
And objective two,

263
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:52,784
build and test Cloud Cart.

264
00:10:53,334 --> 00:10:55,403
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

265
00:11:05,953 --> 00:11:10,540
Next: faster and safer pipelines. Caching, artifacts, and a matrix.

266
00:11:11,090 --> 00:11:14,418
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
